      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Cheilectomy is commonly performed for osteoarthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint and generally has a successful outcome and high rate of patient satisfaction over the short to medium term. Despite the relatively good results achieved in most cases, a proportion of patients have ongoing pain after cheilectomy. This article outlines the potential causes of ongoing pain, including progression of osteoarthritis, neuralgic symptoms, and transfer metatarsalgia. Management strategies for treating the ongoing symptoms are discussed."
      },
